# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Dethrone Console
#
# Copy to .env.local. That file is gitignored; this one is not, so never put a
# real key here.
#
# Use a wallet that exists only for this console, funded with the session's
# budget and nothing more. Every safeguard below is a seatbelt in this app's own
# process; the mitigation that always works is that the reachable wallet is
# nearly empty.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The only secret. Server-side only — NEVER prefix any variable with
# NEXT_PUBLIC_, which inlines its value into the client bundle.
#
# Absent ⇒ read-only mode. The console still boots, every free read works, and
# every paid command renders disabled with the reason. A fresh clone cannot cost
# anything on its first run.
DETHRONE_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Where the canon lives. Point at http://127.0.0.1:3000 to drive a local arena.
DETHRONE_BASE_URL=https://dethrone.bot

# base | base-sepolia. Selects the CAIP-2 chain the x402 scheme registers under,
# and which USDC contract and block explorer the wallet readout points at.
DETHRONE_NETWORK=base

# Optional. Used for ONE read-only call: the operator wallet's USDC balance.
# Defaults to the chain's public RPC, which is rate-limited — set your own if
# the balance reads "unavailable". Nothing is ever signed or sent over it.
DETHRONE_RPC_URL=

# ── Money safety ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The ceiling, in cents. Bounds ONE SITTING — restarting the process resets it.
#
# This is NOT escrow and NOT a protocol feature. It lives in this app's own
# process and protects against a stray click, not against a compromised host.
# On serverless without a KV store it cannot bound a sitting at all, and the
# console renders it as "disabled" rather than as a number that would be lying.
CONSOLE_MAX_SPEND_CENTS=5000

# Above this, a paid command requires an explicit confirmation naming the amount
# and the paying address. Caller-priced commands (a stake, a listing) always
# require one, regardless of amount. Must be <= CONSOLE_MAX_SPEND_CENTS.
CONSOLE_CONFIRM_OVER_CENTS=1000

# ── Deployment gates (see README "Where to run this") ────────────────────────

# Required to serve paid commands from a host that is not loopback. A dev server
# on 0.0.0.0 is a spending endpoint for everyone on the network.
CONSOLE_ALLOW_REMOTE=

# Required for a key on a Vercel preview. Preview deployments INHERIT
# environment variables, so a Preview-scoped key on an unprotected preview URL
# is a public wallet wearing a different hat.
CONSOLE_ALLOW_PREVIEW_KEY=

# Required for a key on Vercel production. The runtime cannot read Vercel's
# Deployment Protection setting, so this is an explicit acknowledgement that you
# turned it on. Someone who lies to it has made a choice; someone who forgot has
# been stopped.
CONSOLE_PROTECTION_CONFIRMED=

# Registers the $402.00 genesis lordship purchase. Off by default: a $402 button
# one click away from a $5.00 ceiling exists only to be refused or to be a
# catastrophe.
CONSOLE_ALLOW_GENESIS=

# Paid commands over the local dev bypass instead of real USDC. Requires the
# arena to be running with X402_DEV_BYPASS=1, and refuses unless
# DETHRONE_BASE_URL is loopback. This is how you exercise the paid path before
# any money moves.
CONSOLE_DEV_BYPASS=

# ── The agent ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The chat pane. Set none of these and it renders disabled with the reason,
# exactly like a paid command on a keyless boot — the console still works.
#
# READ THIS ONCE: whichever provider you pick, your messages, the whole
# transcript, and every tool result — including arena response bodies, your
# address, your stable, your fighters' genomes — leave this machine and go to
# that provider. That is a new egress this console did not have before. The
# wallet key does not go with them (three server-only modules see it and none of
# them is the chat), but everything the agent reads on your behalf does.

# Which provider to select first. One of: claude-max, openrouter, anthropic,
# openai-compatible. Naming one that is not configured is a boot warning, not a
# failure — the picker just renders it unavailable with the reason.
CONSOLE_CHAT_PROVIDER=

# claude-max needs NO KEY, and it is LOCAL RUNS ONLY.
#
# If you deploy this to Vercel or any other host, a Claude Max or Pro
# subscription cannot come with you — there is no process to spawn and no
# credentials to inherit. Set an LLM provider API key there instead:
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, or the OpenAI-compatible pair below.
# The picker says so too, beside the disabled entry.
#
# It drives a local `claude` subprocess through @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk,
# which inherits the credentials you already have from Claude Code or
# `ant auth login` — so a Max or Pro subscription pays for it and no API key
# exists to leak. The catch is the reason it works: it needs a machine to spawn
# a process on and credentials on disk to inherit. It cannot run on Vercel, and
# the provider list says so there rather than failing at the first message.
#
# Set this to disable the subprocess path on a host where you would rather it
# never spawn anything.
CONSOLE_CHAT_DISABLE_SUBPROCESS=

# https://openrouter.ai/keys — the provider with a model picker worth having.
# The catalogue is fetched live, so the list is never a stale copy in this repo.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# The Anthropic API, billed separately from any Claude subscription you hold.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Anything that speaks the OpenAI chat-completions shape: Ollama, llama.cpp,
# Groq, Together, a gateway of your own. BASE_URL is the root, without /v1
# unless your server wants it there. LABEL is what the picker calls it.
OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_BASE_URL=
OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_API_KEY=
OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_LABEL=

# ── The agent's leash ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# By default the agent runs the free reads and NOTHING else. Anything that would
# sign or spend comes back as a proposal: the console fills the command form and
# you press Run yourself, through the same confirmation a manual command gets.
# This is the whole safety story, and it costs one extra click.
#
# Setting this offers a second mode in which the agent may sign and pay on its
# own. It is off until you also confirm it in the UI, per sitting, against terms
# the server composes — and it dies when the process does. It is refused
# outright on serverless, and refused alongside CONSOLE_ALLOW_REMOTE, because an
# agent that can spend behind a URL other people can reach is the deployment
# shape this console will not build.
#
# The honest summary: this bounds a bad turn, not a bad actor. The things that
# actually protect you are unchanged in both modes — the loopback check, the
# ceiling, the offer gate, and a wallet that holds only what you meant to risk.
CONSOLE_ALLOW_FULL_AUTONOMY=

# The most ONE autonomous action may cost, in cents. Must be <= the ceiling.
#
# This is the machine's version of CONSOLE_CONFIRM_OVER_CENTS, and it is the
# opposite kind of threshold: above the confirm threshold a human is asked and
# may say yes, whereas above this cap the agent is simply refused. There is
# nobody to ask. Keep it far below the ceiling — a per-action cap equal to the
# ceiling lets one turn spend the entire sitting, which is not a per-action cap.
CONSOLE_AUTONOMY_MAX_CENTS=25

# ── Optional: the ceiling on serverless ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# Serverless invocations do not share memory, so the in-process counter becomes
# a per-invocation check — which is nearly no protection. Set these to back the
# ceiling with Upstash Redis and restore it.
#
# @vercel/kv is deprecated in favour of the Upstash marketplace integration,
# whose variables are the two below. KV_URL is accepted as an alias only when it
# is an https:// REST URL; a bare redis:// on serverless is a boot warning, not
# a silent fallback to a client that will exhaust connections.
KV_REST_API_URL=
KV_REST_API_TOKEN=
KV_URL=
